 it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62173434-9d78-53c4-8dd5-2f7d9a6049d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359298} {:answer/id #uuid "9a46ad2c-3221-509b-85ab-e1f6a21f5a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359299} {:answer/id #uuid "2fabd81f-db54-548c-8859-03aae9b18d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359300} {:answer/id #uuid "969acbba-2c72-54ed-aa3c-a4015f0fd659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359301} {:answer/id #uuid "21725338-5db3-5130-8ccc-94491ba1335e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a46ad2c-3221-509b-85ab-e1f6a21f5a12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359299} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1c38346-bc92-53ea-ada3-765f33ddc95c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d72cebfd-0272-53df-afde-56fca4255636" :question/math-academy-id "q-71814" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x<y<{x}^{2},\\,1<x\\le 2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }2<y<x}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }4\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b80ebf0-a04f-566a-8a22-11ff8c27d655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359305} {:answer/id #uuid "39b4afff-1070-5f5e-8e9b-069710df28ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359306} {:answer/id #uuid "454e3492-f330-5f6a-8fb5-3e94d873a156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359307} {:answer/id #uuid "2f90083c-dae3-59c9-b9fd-c319037c0e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359308} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb480d1-7eea-5baf-ad60-66e5d8409d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39b4afff-1070-5f5e-8e9b-069710df28ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359306} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab7e22db-a563-577d-b069-6fd85b2e89a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a7a02d7-66a2-5603-90a7-24ad44988e9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-71819" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<1,\\,{y}^{2}<4}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
